Z||000000||Philphine||05-26-2001||05:24 PM||||<BR>is there a good, or maybe more important, bad time of year to get a paint job? i just got my last major body piece on my '87 coupe and after trying to smooth out and line up pieces i'll be about ready for paint. if it got too far into summer or fall (i don't work too fast) should i wait till spring to do it. i have no paint experience but it just seems like winter or fall would be a bad time to have a car painted. i don't have a garage so it would be exposed to what ever is going on weatherwise.||||reg||1
Z||000001||AusFiero||05-26-2001||07:57 PM||||No real good or bad time really. Most of it will depend on humidity or moisture levels in the air.<P>Painting outside can be a real *****  though. You will be suprised how many bugs and critters are attracted to new paint.<P>You didn't mention if you was using acrylic, enamel or a 2 pack paint. If you have to paint outside, use acrylic, it is more forgiving.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>When I was young I spent 80% of my money on Women, Gambling and Alcohol. I guess I wasted the rest.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||05-26-2001||09:07 PM||||i wouldn't be doing the painting.i'd have to get a local shop to do it. i guess i'm wondering about how i've heard the paint has to cure or something. i know you don't wax them for some time after, but i don't know how tempeture affects the process.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||05-27-2001||07:03 AM||||No time is really preferable. I just tell people if they ask, do it rite after winter, when everyone is done sliding on snow and ice and running into you. Then its brand new for the spring/summer driving season.||||reg||1
